      "Children": [
        {
          "$type": "Models.PMF.Plant, Models",
          "PlantType": "IntermediateWheatgrass",
          "Name": "IntermediateWheatgrass",
          "ResourceName": null,
          "Children": [
            {
              "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
              "Text": "_Peter Innes, University of Southern Queensland_\n\n\n## Introduction\n\nPerennial cropping potentially brings a wide range of environmental and economic benefits. Land sown to perennial pastures have been shown to have higher soil organic carbon levels than land regularly cultivated for cropping (Badgery et al. 2014). A comparison of an annual wheat variety with a wild perennial relative, Intermediate Wheat Grass, illustrated that the wheat grass is deeper rooted than annual wheat and thus can access deeper layers of nutrients and soil water, lessening the risk of nutrient leaching and water table rise (Crews, Carton & Olsson 2018; Culman et al. 2013; DeHaan & Ismail 2017; Duchene et al. 2020; Glover et al. 2010; Soto-Gómez & Pérez-Rodríguez 2022). Another of the perceived benefits of perennial crops is the ability to capture precipitation year-round, unlike annuals which have a relatively short growing season and often leave the soil bare and susceptible to water and wind erosion. Maintaining a perennial crop requires less tillage and spraying than annual cropping, resulting in less machinery operational costs and reduced soil compaction.  .\n\r\n## Presentation\r\n\r\nThis model has been built using the Plant Modelling Framework (PMF) of [brown_plant_2014] to simulate the growth of perennial grains as a grain and forage crop.  The perennial grain model simulates the above ground plant structure, including the photosynthesis process, using the SimpleLeaf procedure of PMF, which does not consider explicitly leaf age or placement in the canopy.  The model describes a perennial crop, with phenology rewinding to the vegetative stage at the end of the reproductive phase.\r\n\r\n\r\n## Inclusion in APSIM simulations\r\n It is recommended that new Intemediate Wheatgrass simulations are based on the example simulation in the APSIM \"open an example\" tab;\r\n To become active and grow, the perennial grain crop needs to be sown using a manager script with a sowing rule. e.g.:\r\n<pre><code class='language-cs'>\r\n 2022-05-22 IntermediateWheatgrass.Sow(population: 50, cultivar: IntermediateWheatgrass, depth: 20, rowSpacing: 150);  \r\n</code></pre>\r\nIf a specified cultivar is not available, a fatal error will be thrown.\r\n\r\n\r\n### Harvest and biomass removal\r\nPerennial grain biomass can be removed by scheduling a biomass removal event, such as harvesting or cutting.  The proportion of the biomass of each organ that is removed from the system and/or added to the residue pools may be specified in a manager script. All parameters are optional, defaults are used whenever any value is not specified.\r\n\r\n\r\n### Crop termination\r\nTo fully terminate a crop the EndCrop event should be raised:\r\n<pre><code class='language-cs'> IntermediateWheatgrass.EndCrop();  \r\n</code></pre>\r\nOnce a crop has been ended the field is open to be used by another APSIM plant model, or another perennial grain crop.\r\n",
              "Name": "GeneralDescription",
              "ResourceName": null,
              "Children": [],
              "Enabled": true,
              "ReadOnly": false
            },
            {
