                {
                  "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                  "Text": "The phenology includes nine phases from Germinating to Harvest, with each phase starting and ending at one of nine stages. The transition between the Germination to Emerging, Emerging to Vernalisation, Reproductive to Grainfilling, and Grainfilling to HarvestRipe phases are governed by thermal time. Like many other cool climate grasses, intermediate wheatgrass requires dual-induction by vernalisation and photoperiod (increasing daylength) before proceeding to reproduction (Heide, 1994). This entails a period of cool temperatures (<5°C) and short days (<11 hours), i.e. vernalisation, followed by long days (>13 hours), i.e. secondary induction (Duchene et al., 2021; Heide, 1994; Ivancic et al., 2021; Locatelli et al., 2022). The transition from Vernalising to Inducing is controlled by a vernalisation days factor (accounting for low temperature duration). The transition from Inducing to Reproductive is controlled primarily by daylength (photoperiod). The transition from Reproductive to GrainFilling is slowed by both high temperature (Cicchino et al., 2010), and leaf moisture stress ([Leaf].FW) (Chauhan et al., 2019).",
                  "Name": "Description",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [],
                  "Enabled": true,
                  "ReadOnly": false
                },
                {
                  "$type": "Models.Functions.SubDailyInterpolation, Models",
                  "agregationMethod": 0,
                  "Name": "ThermalTime",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                      "Text": "The thermal time is used to control many of the phenological changes in perennial grain.  It is calculated using the daily average temperature and three parameters, or cardinal temperatures: minimum, maximum, and optimum.  Plant development accelerates as temperature increases from minimum to optimum and slows down after that, ceasing completely at maximum temperature.\r\n",
                      "Name": "Description",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.XYPairs, Models",
                      "X": [
                        0.0,
                        25.0,
                        35.0,
                        40.0
                      ],
                      "Y": [
                        0.0,
                        30.0,
                        25.0,
                        0.0
                      ],
                      "Name": "Response",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
