                {
                  "$type": "Models.PMF.Phen.GenericPhase, Models",
                  "Start": "Emergence",
                  "End": "Induction",
                  "IsEmerged": true,
                  "Name": "Vernalising",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                      "Text": "During this phase, perennial grain plants allocates new biomass to only leaf+petiole, stems, and root organs. Initially, this phase starts when plants emerge, but it is also triggered as phenology resets after the end of the reproductive phase.  The vegetative phase ends when the inductive phase starts.  \r\nperennial grain is a long day species, with flowering starting in spring when day light is increasing  \n",
                      "Name": "Description",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.Constant, Models",
                      "FixedValue": 49.0,
                      "Units": "days",
                      "Name": "Target",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.VariableReference, Models",
                      "VariableName": "[Phenology].Vernalisation.TodaysVernalisation",
                      "Name": "Progression",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    }
                  ],
                  "Enabled": true,
                  "ReadOnly": false
                },
                {
                  "$type": "Models.PMF.Phen.GenericPhase, Models",
                  "Start": "Induction",
                  "End": "StemElongation",
                  "IsEmerged": true,
                  "Name": "Inducing",
                  "ResourceName": null,
                  "Children": [
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Memo, Models",
                      "Text": "During this phase the perennial grain plants await for the environmental cue to start the reproductive growth.  Physiologic and metabolic processes remain the same as for the vegetative phase with plants allocating biomass to leaf+petiole, stems, and root organs.  As perennial grain requires long days for flowering, the trigger to end this phase is defined by the length of day, or more specifically by the increase in daily photoperiod.  The plants need a few days under favourable conditions, photoperiod increasing and greater than a threshold, to start the reproductive phase.\r\n",
                      "Name": "Description",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
                    {
                      "$type": "Models.Functions.Constant, Models",
                      "FixedValue": 10.0,
                      "Units": "days",
                      "Name": "Target",
                      "ResourceName": null,
                      "Children": [],
                      "Enabled": true,
                      "ReadOnly": false
                    },
